      ":question/id": "69c1fa40-c0c9-5012-be30-2eaa523c6adb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(5+x^2){y}^{′}+4y={e}^{x},\\,y(4)=3$\n- **II.** $(5+x^2){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(2)={y}^{′}(2)=-2$\n- **III.** $(4+x^2){y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(5+x^2){y}^{′}+4y={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(5+x^2){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)={y}^{′}(2)=-2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1f24b0e9-6f87-55e8-964f-77f4fd23e395",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
